,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2184.221445915,"data":"        \"output\": \"I remember the vertical and horizontal Rust shapes from Ordo back in 2022. A vertical Rust shape means a message first flows through a layer's incoming edge, then across the layer diagonal, then through the layer outgoing edge, preserving the layer index. A horizontal Rust shape means the message flows through all incoming edges in order, then through the first outgoing edge, and returns nil when there are no more outgoing edges. The sketch shows the layer graph with message flow lines and Rust-trace bands. The Rust shape is determined by the flow through layers and threads. If a message moves vertically across a layer diagonal, it forms a vertical Rust band. If it moves only horizontally between peers, it forms a horizontal Rust band. The shape still shows the layer order but not the thread id. I can remember the Ordo shapes because I built them in Rust. The behavior stayed close to the design even when I tried different async/runtime choices. I did not use async channels or join()\u003esplit(\\\"\u003e\\\") routing at first. The message stayed a plain String on the ground bus, and the layers were plain route edges. The Rust structs formed automatically from the graph edges. Then I tried threads by wrapping each ground bus trip in a new ThreadLocal\u003cString\u003e. That added the thread id to the trace\"\n"}
